/* Curated group exhibit: anonymous servant letters, 1889, Britain. */
window.MUSEUM_QUIET_EXHIBITS=window.MUSEUM_QUIET_EXHIBITS||[];
window.MUSEUM_QUIET_EXHIBITS.push({
  "id": "victorian-domestic-servants",
  "year": "1870s–1890s · Britain",
  "zhYear": "十九世纪后期 · 英国",
  "type": "Gender · Class · Domestic service",
  "zhType": "女性 · 阶级 · 家庭雇佣",
  "title": "The Women Who Asked Not to Be Named",
  "zhTitle": "她们写下了不满，却不敢留下姓名",
  "subtitle": "Domestic service, dependence and the private price of a complaint",
  "zhSubtitle": "在别人的屋檐下工作，也在别人的屋檐下生活",
  "object": "Anonymous domestic servants' letters published in 1889",
  "zhObject": "1889年刊出的匿名女佣来信",
  "sources": [
    [
      "Toilers in London (1889), chapter XIII: Letters from Servants",
      "https://www.victorianlondon.org/publications3/newtoilers-13.htm"
    ],
    [
      "Domestic Service: The 'Mute and Forgotten' Occupation",
      "https://www.victorianweb.org/history/work/burnett3.html"
    ],
    [
      "The History Press: Women and Domestic Service in Victorian Society",
      "https://thehistorypress.co.uk/article/women-and-domestic-service-in-victorian-society/"
    ]
  ],
  "en": [
    "In 1889 a collection of correspondence from servants appeared in an investigation of female labour in London. One woman described a painful knee and the demands of scrubbing a kitchen floor; she also pleaded with the investigator not to identify her. Another worried that if her mistress discovered her complaint, she would be dismissed.",
    "Domestic service was one of the largest forms of employment open to women in Victorian Britain. The household offered work, wages, often board, and often lodging, but it also put a young employee under the authority of the people whose home she occupied. The place she slept could also be the place she was ordered to work. Leaving an unhappy position did not necessarily mean returning to a secure home, especially for women who had travelled from rural districts and lacked relatives or independent income in the city.",
    "In one of the 1889 letters, a servant said she had come to London after being promised treatment like a member of the family. The reality described in her account was long working hours, very low annual pay, and restrictions on going out. Another described the conflict between her own religious practice and the church attendance expected by an employer. It became a struggle over whose convictions could govern another person's Sunday.",
    "Victorian respectable households were built upon boundaries often invisible to visitors. A family might regard itself as generous because a servant lived under its roof. Yet the same arrangement could make privacy scarce and turn disagreement into a threat to employment and accommodation. The power imbalance was not always enforced through spectacular violence. Sometimes it appeared in the authority to decide when another person could visit friends, rest, eat, worship, or go outside.",
    "She would need somewhere else to live and another employer willing to hire her. References and reputation mattered in a market where households made judgments about a worker's reliability. A complaint might follow her into the search for the next position.",
    "Household employment also fragmented workers. A factory employee might see scores of colleagues during a shift; a maid in a small household could spend much of her day with only one other servant, or alone. Comparing grievances, organising collective action, and discovering that others felt the same way could therefore be difficult. This was a structural condition, not proof that domestic workers lacked courage. The very existence of the 1889 correspondence demonstrates that women could recognise injustice and seek an audience when an intermediary promised secrecy.",
    "The social language of the period complicated the situation further. Employers might present obedience as good character and domestic service as a respectable path for working-class girls. A worker seeking a future job might have reason to appear grateful and steady even while believing that a particular employer behaved badly. Shame, class prejudice and the possibility of being called troublesome could enter the calculation. Yet the same letter writers also insisted on ordinary human dignity. Their words are evidence of disagreement, not a portrait of uniform docility.",
    "Nor was religion a single explanation for silence. The published letters contain differing Christian affiliations and references to religious intermediaries who helped grievances reach an editor. Faith could be used to insist upon duty, but religious networks also offered a route for complaint. A useful account asks which institution interpreted the moral rule, who had authority to enforce it, and whether the servant had an alternative community willing to listen. Reducing the subject to 'Victorian religion' would conceal the very contradictions that the evidence exposes.",
    "Education made some differences visible. Reading an advertisement, writing a letter, recognising the value of a testimonial, and understanding an employer's expectations could change a worker's options. But literacy was not the same as power. A servant could write clearly and still fear that the sentence would cost her position. The 1889 collection survived in part because editors, investigators and intermediaries created a channel unavailable to every servant at every moment.",
    "These letters also require careful reading. They were selected for publication by investigators who had their own commitments. Not every servant wrote, not every letter survived, and not every household resembled the worst examples described. It may be evidence of resistance shaped by a need to survive its consequences.",
    "Letters from London domestic servants published in 1889 document wages, long working hours, illness and restrictions on private life. Some requested anonymity because disclosure could endanger their employment. Live-in service placed working arrangements, accommodation and reputational assessments under the authority of the employing household."
  ],
  "zh": [
    "1889年刊出的伦敦女佣来信，记载了工作时间、工资、身体不适和私人活动受到限制等情况。有写信者请求调查者不要公开姓名，并说明身份暴露可能导致被雇主解雇。调查者与编辑公开刊登了部分来信，匿名要求也因此保存下来。",
    "十九世纪的英国，家庭佣工是许多女性可以找到的重要工作。对于从乡下进城的年轻女孩，一户人家可能同时意味着工资、饭食和住处。她每天在那里工作，晚上也在那里睡觉。雇主不只决定她做什么，还可能决定她什么时候休息、能不能出去见朋友、是否有机会照顾自己的身体。一个人对工作的不满，因而很难与她整个生活分开。",
    "有位女佣在来信中回忆，自己曾被许诺会像家庭成员一样受到照顾。等真正到了伦敦，等待她的却是漫长的工作时间和极低的年薪。还有人提到，她所属的教派与主人家不同，却依然被要求按照雇主的安排参与宗教活动。这里的宗教并不是一句『当时的人都相信什么』便能解释的。",
    "离开意味着另找工作，可能还意味着失去住处。她必须预先承担那句话之后的一连串事情。",
    "这些女佣之间也未必有机会互相认识。一个工厂里的人可以看到许多工友，而住在一户小家庭里的佣工，也许整日只有一两个同伴。即使每个人都觉得不公平，也未必知道别人是否怀着同样的想法。有时候，沉默并不是所有人的共识，而是每个人都误以为只有自己不愿继续忍受。能够相互确认、彼此支持，本身就需要一个人无法独自创造的条件。",
    "当时社会对『好女佣』有许多期待。如果雇主把一切要求都包装成对一个年轻女孩的教导，那么她想保护自己的权益时，便可能先被指责不够懂事。",
    "然而，宗教与道德并不只站在雇主那一边。1889年的信件有些就是通过关心佣工处境的宗教人士传递出去的。相同的信仰语言，可以被用来要求人服从，也可以成为帮助弱者说话的理由。因此，不能说『宗教让她们沉默』。",
    "识字同样不能自动改变处境。能够写信的人，至少多了一种表达工具；但她写得再清楚，也未必有能力承担雇主的报复。那几封进入报刊的信，并不是所有女佣的共同经历。没有这些人，纸上的话或许根本无法抵达公众面前。",
    "有人讲自己膝盖疼痛，仍然需要跪下来擦洗厨房；有人描述主人如何限制她见朋友；有人说，如果让女主人知道自己写了信，她就会被解雇。这些话没有宏大的宣言，也没有要求一群人马上走上街头。",
    "有人能够换工作，有人得到过体面的待遇，也有人留下了直接的投诉。这是建立在制度与现存书信上的解释，而不是可以替每位女佣确认的心理活动。",
    "1889年刊出的伦敦女佣来信，记载了工资、劳动时间、身体不适和私人活动受到限制等情况。有些写信人明确要求匿名，以免被雇主认出并失去工作。住家雇佣使劳动、食宿和雇主评价集中在同一户家庭，部分女佣通过调查者与编辑才得以公开自己的经历。",
    "家庭佣工与工厂劳动者的工作空间不同。许多住家女佣的休息、饮食与活动范围由雇主家庭安排，她们与其他劳动者接触的机会也受到生活地点影响。女佣来信中的工作时长、健康问题以及休息安排，为研究这种雇佣制度提供了具体记录。"
  ],
  "mechanisms": [
    "housing tied to employment",
    "fear of dismissal and references",
    "class and gender expectations",
    "fragmented workplaces",
    "employer control over worship and privacy"
  ],
  "zhMechanisms": [
    "工作与住处捆绑",
    "失业与求职证明",
    "阶级与性别规范",
    "同伴之间的隔离",
    "雇主对私人生活的控制"
  ],
  "question": "What does a complaint cost when the person who hears it also controls your work and home?",
  "zhQuestion": "当决定你工作和住处的人，也是你想要投诉的人，开口究竟要付出什么代价？",
  "limits": "The 1889 letters are selected, mediated testimony. They show actual fears of identification but cannot represent every servant or reveal unwritten decisions.",
  "zhLimits": "1889年的书信经过调查者选择与发表，能够证明部分女佣对身份暴露的具体顾虑，不能代表所有佣工，也不能替没有留下文字的人发言。",
  "methodEn": "This exhibit centres on surviving anonymous letters and on the organisation of domestic service. It does not turn a public protest into the ending or invent interior dialogue for undocumented women.",
  "methodZh": "这份档案以女佣留下的匿名书信和家庭雇佣制度为中心。我们不替没有留下证词的人写心理独白，也不把反抗运动当作故事必须抵达的结尾。"
});

;
/* END museum-quiet-servants.js */

