window.MUSEUM_QUIET_EXHIBITS.push({
  "id": "colonial-indian-widows",
  "year": "Late 18th–19th century · North India",
  "zhYear": "十八世纪末至十九世纪 · 印度北部",
  "type": "Widowhood · Religion · Family law",
  "zhType": "丧偶女性 · 宗教习俗 · 家庭法律",
  "title": "A Life After Her Husband's Death",
  "zhTitle": "丈夫去世后，她的人生为什么也被留在原地？",
  "subtitle": "Widowhood, family authority, inheritance and the ordinary boundaries of refusal",
  "zhSubtitle": "寡妇身份、家族权威与一种被规定好的人生",
  "object": "Anglo-Hindu legal records and scholarship on widows in Banaras",
  "zhObject": "贝拿勒斯地区寡妇财产诉讼与殖民时期法律记录",
  "sources": [
    [
      "Rochisha Narayan, Widows, Family, Community and Anglo-Hindu Law, Modern Asian Studies",
      "https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/modern-asian-studies/article/abs/widows-family-community-and-the-formation-of-anglohindu-law-in-eighteenthcentury-india/A79A4097DCEF67050CA595605BE5F2E8"
    ],
    [
      "Oxford Academic: Hindu widows, law, religion and marriage",
      "https://academic.oup.com/book/45654/chapter/398017768"
    ],
    [
      "Cambridge University Press: Can religious norms undermine property rights?",
      "https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/british-journal-of-political-science/article/british-academy-brian-barry-prize-essay-can-religious-norms-undermine-effective-property-rights-evidence-from-inheritance-rights-of-widows-in-colonial-india/626A2F77E23D810EAAC438749DA78034"
    ]
  ],
  "en": [
    "A husband died; the woman who survived him did not thereby disappear. Yet in parts of eighteenth- and nineteenth-century North India, widowhood could become a social position that narrowed what a woman was expected to wear, whom she could marry, how she could live within a household, and what authority she possessed over property. To the women themselves, daily life involved a larger and more uneven set of relations: property, kinship, religious expectation, caste, the claims of children, and the danger of losing a place to belong.",
    "It would be a serious mistake to call all Indian widows a single oppressed class with identical customs. Practices varied by caste, locality, wealth, family structure and time. High-caste Hindu conventions restricting remarriage were influential in certain settings, while other communities had different practices. Some widows owned or managed resources, and some pursued property claims in courts. The issue is therefore not whether Indian women as a whole lacked agency. It is how specific systems placed unusually high costs on certain forms of choice, and how a woman whose social world was organised through kinship might decide what demands were possible to make.",
    "Religious explanation needs particular care. Brahmanical legal and social ideas contributed to rules concerning marriage, widowhood and inheritance. But a religious text is not a complete description of what happened in every village or household. Local custom, bargaining within families, and property disputes affected how those ideas were applied. Colonial officials, meanwhile, tried to give legal force to particular interpretations of scriptural tradition. To describe the entire situation simply as a timeless religious prohibition would hide the role of British administration and the historical process by which practices acquired the force of official law.",
    "Research on the Banaras region shows that late eighteenth-century colonial agrarian and judicial reforms could disadvantage widows' access to resources. Rochisha Narayan's study traces women navigating inheritance and property disputes and describes how Anglo-Hindu legal categories narrowed the position available to them. This is valuable precisely because it complicates the image of passive women. Some widows were litigants and resourceful negotiators. Their cases, however, also reveal a less theatrical form of pressure: obtaining recognition could require understanding rules established by institutions that did not necessarily treat a woman's lived relationship with property as the same as a man's.",
    "The answer could begin in the household. A widow might need to live with relatives whose support was intertwined with the inheritance she wished to dispute. A confrontation could make relations with in-laws difficult and endanger practical arrangements for herself or for children. She might possess a moral objection but lack money for legal help, means of travel or someone trustworthy to represent her. These are historically plausible constraints. They are not proof that an unnamed woman felt the same fear or reached the same calculation.",
    "Family reputation was another form of power, though it rarely left behind a regulation as clear as a court statute. To challenge an inheritance might be cast as greed; to seek remarriage might be treated as a breach of duty or familial honour in communities where remarriage was discouraged. The strength of these expectations depended on social position. An action that looked like ordinary independence to one woman could threaten another with exclusion from relatives upon whom she depended. Social norms become powerful not only when someone shouts a command, but when people learn to anticipate what everyone else will think.",
    "The most difficult form of silence to examine is internal. A woman who had grown up within a system of duties might not describe every act of restraint as unjust. She might regard some responsibilities as worthwhile and others as painful, without dividing her life into the simple categories of consent and coercion. Religious practice could be a source of meaning, not only a system of control.",
    "Education mattered, but not in the crude sense that a person had to be ignorant in order to submit. Women with limited literacy could negotiate through family, community networks and oral knowledge. Literate women could still face legal disadvantages. The more concrete question is whether a widow could read the papers that affected her resources, afford an adviser, reach a court, or communicate her case to someone with authority. Information expands possibilities only when a person also possesses a practical means of making it count.",
    "The presence of reform debates in nineteenth-century India is not the natural ending for this particular room. Some campaigns sought to revise the law; others defended established customs, and individual circumstances continued to differ after legal measures changed. The number of public legal cases is not a census of those who quietly endured, any more than a lack of litigation proves agreement with family decisions.",
    "Colonial records are also selective witnesses. They tend to foreground people whose disputes reached administrative institutions, especially disputes substantial enough to leave traces on paper. A woman who settled a disagreement privately, postponed it, or had no safe means to pursue it may have left little documentation. The silence is most visible as an absence produced by unequal access to the means of formal argument.",
    "In North India, colonial reforms combined administrative techniques with European ideas, locally influential religious interpretations and existing hierarchies of caste and property. More elaborate courts did not automatically secure more freedom for every woman. Institutions could formalise disadvantages as well as resolve disputes.",
    "Widowhood in North India differed by caste, locality, family property and household ties. Some inheritance and residence disputes reached colonial courts; others were settled outside formal litigation. Colonial interpretations of Hindu law interacted with local custom and kinship arrangements in defining women's recorded legal positions."
  ],
  "zh": [
    "丈夫去世了，留下来的女人却未必能够重新决定自己的生活。在十八世纪末到十九世纪的印度北部，一些女性从成为寡妇的那一天起，就要面对另一套关于衣着、生活、婚姻和财产的期待。她仍然是一个人，仍然需要吃饭、生活，也可能有自己的愿望；只是这些愿望能否实现，往往要先经过亲属、社群和法律的认可。",
    "不同地区、种姓、家庭和年代的习惯并不一样。在某些高种姓印度教社群中，再婚受到严格限制；另一些群体则有不同的传统。有些寡妇能够管理财产，有些甚至走进法庭为自己争取权益。",
    "宗教是其中的一部分，却不是全部。某些宗教法律与家庭伦理规定了寡妇应有的身份和责任，地方习俗和亲属关系又决定这些规定如何进入日常。后来英国殖民政府试图把特定的宗教文本解释纳入法律体系，使一些原本能够在家庭或地方协商的关系，变成由官方规定和法庭认定的身份。于是，压迫并不只是古老传统的延续，它也可能在新的行政制度里获得更稳定的形式。",
    "研究印度贝拿勒斯地区的历史学者发现，殖民时期的土地与司法改革影响了女性取得和控制财产的机会。有寡妇试图争取继承权，也有人通过熟悉的关系网络处理纠纷。但对另一些没有财力、没有支持者的女性来说，走进法庭未必是现实的选项。",
    "当时社会对于『守节』『体面』和家庭荣誉的看法，也可能使反对变得困难。一个女人想再婚，可能被解释成不守本分；她想争取财产，也可能被亲属指责贪心。最令人窒息的地方，有时不是有人明确地禁止，而是一个人早已知道，说出愿望之后会听见什么评价。为了继续生活在熟悉的关系里，她也许只能把愿望收回去。",
    "不同寡妇对宗教义务与家族责任的理解，无法由法律文书作出统一判断。一个人可能真诚地重视家庭责任与宗教信仰，也可能同时对某些具体要求感到痛苦。",
    "识字可能帮助一个人看懂契约、理解继承规则，知道哪些权利正在被讨论。但她即使读得懂文件，也未必有钱去请律师；她即使明白自己的处境，也未必敢让家里的人知道她想提出要求。知识与行动之间，还有经济、身份和权力的距离。",
    "历史上确实发生过改变法律的行动，但改变法律之后，有些女人仍然要在原有的亲属关系里生活。对她们而言，人生没有从一个明确的日期开始变得简单。",
    "档案不会回答所有问题。能够留下文字的，往往是进入诉讼或行政记录的纠纷。",
    "十八世纪末至十九世纪印度北部的寡妇，因地区、种姓、财产和亲属关系而面对不同的婚姻及继承安排。部分争议进入殖民法院，留下财产诉讼记录；另一些家庭决定则未形成正式案件。殖民机构对印度教法律的解释与地方习惯共同影响了这些女性的法律地位。",
    "殖民时期的法庭主要根据诉讼内容记录财产与继承纠纷。妇女参与案件的方式并不相同，有人作为权利主张者进入法庭，也有人由亲属代为处理争议。土地、房屋与其他财产的归属，通常还涉及夫家、子女及更广泛的亲族关系。法院对某一争议的裁决，能够改变当事人的法律位置，却不意味着所有家庭都采用相同的继承与居住安排。"
  ],
  "mechanisms": [
    "kinship dependence",
    "religious and caste-specific norms",
    "colonial legal categories",
    "property and inheritance inequality",
    "reputation and social belonging"
  ],
  "zhMechanisms": [
    "对亲属关系的依赖",
    "宗教解释与种姓习惯",
    "殖民法律的身份规定",
    "财产与继承不平等",
    "社会名誉与归属"
  ],
  "question": "What if the people you rely on to survive are also those whose rules you would have to refuse?",
  "zhQuestion": "如果维持生活必须依靠的人，恰恰也是你需要反对的人，那么拒绝究竟意味着什么？",
  "limits": "Customs varied by caste, region and century; some widows litigated. The archival absence of others does not prove consent or private plans to rebel.",
  "zhLimits": "不同种姓、地区和年代的习俗并不相同；部分寡妇曾诉讼。没有诉讼记录，不等于同意压迫，也不能证明她曾考虑反抗。",
  "methodEn": "A social-historical interpretation of widowhood and family law, not a fictional shared widow's voice. Particular examples are distinguished from the wider structural inquiry.",
  "methodZh": "本展品考察寡妇身份与家庭法律的历史关系，不虚构一位能代表所有人的寡妇，也不借某一次改革来替她们的人生收尾。"
});

;
/* END museum-quiet-widows.js */

